From: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp@gmail.com>
Subject: econet-eth: back off the NAPI poll timers under load

The TX-done poll hrtimer (150 us) and the RX watchdog hrtimer (1 ms,
which fired napi_schedule unconditionally every tick) were needed to
work around the unreliable QDMA done-queue / RX-DONE interrupts for low
traffic. Under sustained real traffic, however, the hardware interrupts
do fire (econet IRQ advances ~58k over a download), so these timers
become redundant overhead: on an Archer C5v running PPPoE they produced
~350k timer interrupts during a single speed test, ~71% softirq with
27% CPU still idle (throughput latency-bound, not CPU-bound), an
"hrtimer: interrupt took 22470 ns" warning and an eventual
"sched: RT throttling activated" with a momentary ethernet/Wi-Fi stall.

Two conservative changes:
  - Stretch the TX-done poll from 150 us to 500 us. The 128-entry TX
    ring still cannot fill within that window even at the driver's peak
    (~21 kpps -> ~6 ms to fill), so throughput is unaffected while the
    timer-interrupt rate drops ~3x.
  - Only napi_schedule from the RX watchdog when the ring actually has
    unprocessed descriptors. Under load the real RX-DONE IRQ already
    drained it, so the unconditional wake just burned a softirq per
    tick; the poll is still there for packets the edge-only IRQ missed.

--- a/econet_qdma.c
+++ b/econet_qdma.c
@@ -444,7 +444,7 @@
  * Instead of relying on the hardware, poll for completions shortly
  * after every submission and keep polling while anything is in
  * flight. */
-#define EN75_TX_DONE_KICK_NS	150000
+#define EN75_TX_DONE_KICK_NS	500000
 
 static enum hrtimer_restart en75_tx_done_kick(struct hrtimer *t)
 {
@@ -479,7 +479,13 @@
 	if (!READ_ONCE(qdma->users) || READ_ONCE(qdma->destroying))
 		return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
 
-	napi_schedule(&q->napi);
+	/* Only wake NAPI if the ring actually has unprocessed descriptors.
+	 * Under load the real RX-DONE IRQ already drains the ring, so an
+	 * unconditional napi_schedule here just burns a softirq every tick
+	 * and adds scheduler/timer pressure; the poll is only needed for the
+	 * occasional packet the (edge-only) RX-DONE IRQ failed to signal. */
+	if (en75_rreg(&q->qchain_regs->rx_hwi) != (q->cpu_i + 1) % q->ndesc)
+		napi_schedule(&q->napi);
 	hrtimer_forward_now(t, ns_to_ktime(EN75_RX_KICK_NS));
 
 	return HRTIMER_RESTART;
